AB  - The recognition of keratoconus is emerging as a focus of interest in contemporary Ophthalmology, which is important because a timely identification of the disease can prevent severe vision loss and intervene in the disease process, long before it reaches an advanced stage of risk to vision. This study assesses the role of artificial intelligence, namely modern deep learning algorithms, in the development of systems for automated clinician-based classification of corneal disease based on topography images. After precisely labelling a corpus of corneal topography scans, a number of processing techniques, normalization, and augmentation are employed to improve the quality of the topography scans and therefore enhance the efficacy and robustness of the deep learning models. Novel Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) architectures are evaluated, that recognized subtle changes in the shape and curvature of the cornea, which traditional diagnostic techniques may not recognize. The importance of preparation steps which are supported by data preparation, preprocessing and feature extraction phases to identify clinically-relevant features/patterns in the corneal maps, is emphasized. A process for selecting and tuning high performing models: ResNet, EfficientNet and Dense Net, models which can be used on many other medical imaging classification and diagnostic tasks, has been outlined. Validation methods against frameworks with full training and testing of the models have also been provided.
