AB  - Keratoconus is a progressive disease that affects the cornea. It causes thinning and distortion, leading to serious vision problems if not detected and treated early. In this work, we introduce a new multi-modal deep learning framework for early detection and predicting the progression of keratoconus. This framework combines information from various imaging sources and clinical data. Specifically, our system uses corneal topography maps, Scheimpflug tomography scans, optical coherence tomography (OCT) images, and patient-specific clinical features like age, corneal thickness, intraocular pressure, and refraction metrics. Each imaging type is processed through its own sub-network to automatically extract important features. These features are then aligned, combined, and further processed to capture interactions between the different modalities and create comprehensive diagnostic signatures.We perform experiments using a large, multi-center clinical dataset collected from various imaging devices and patient groups. For early detection of keratoconus, our method achieves an area under the ROC curve (AUROC) of 0.98 on test sets, significantly surpassing individual modality baselines like topography or OCT alone. For predicting progression, which means forecasting the severity or rate of corneal change over time, the model reaches 93% accuracy and shows strong performance across different centers and imaging techniques. Our studies indicate that both the imaging modalities and the clinical features play important roles in performance. The fusion architecture clearly outperforms simple concatenation or late fusion methods.Additionally, we examine how interpretable the model is by using saliency maps and attention techniques. These tools highlight important areas, such as thinning regions and curvature peaks, across the different modalities, which helps us understand how the model makes its predictions. Our findings suggest that this integrated, user-focused deep learning tool can assist ophthalmologists in their decisions, potentially leading to earlier interventions, personalized monitoring, and better outcomes for patients at risk for keratoconus.
