AB  - Keratoconus is a degenerative condition of the cornea that may cause considerable visual deterioration. Diagnosing keratoconus remains challenging for ophthalmologists, primarily because it is a time-intensive process and involves complex clinical evaluations. This study introduces a compact convolutional neural network (CNN) architecture specifically developed to analyze both RGB and grayscale tomography images for the purpose of keratoconus detection. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first study to utilize low-depth grayscale images for the detection of keratoconus. The proposed CNN model was applied to classify color-coded maps derived from the Pentacam device. Each map was tested under five different formats—RGB, 8-bit grayscale (256 levels), 4-bit grayscale (16 levels), 3-bit grayscale (8 levels), and 2-bit grayscale (4 levels)—to enable a comprehensive comparison. The model was evaluated and compared to assess the effectiveness of grayscale quantization on keratoconus detection using color-coded maps. Results indicated that the RGB input yielded the lowest performance. Among the grayscale formats, the 8-level grayscale achieved the highest performance when using corneal thickness (CT) or elevation anterior (EA) maps. For sagittal anterior (SAG) maps, both colored images and the 8-level grayscale format yielded the highest classification accuracy. Additional experiments with traditional machine learning (ML) approaches demonstrated variable performance, with certain configurations yielding strong results and others performing less effectively.
