AB  - Keratoconus (KC) is a progressive ocular disease involving thinning and conical steepening of the cornea, which may result in serious visual impairment if left undiagnosed or untreated. It is crucial to diagnose keratoconus early and correctly in order to take appropriate action and prevent the condition from worsening. Traditional diagnostic methods, are slow to respond to new forms of disease, and much time and effort are spent in this process. Although current deep learning (DL) methods have shown high performance in computerized KC detection. This paper introduces CorneaNet, a new multiinput convolutional neural network (CNN) structure for explainable and robust keratoconus classification from Scheimpflug tomography scans. Our model takes four multimodal inputs: anterior curvature, posterior elevation, pachymetry, and wavefront aberration maps. Gradientweighted Class Activation Mapping (Grad-CAM) is integrated into the framework for creating visual heatmaps that identify the pathological features. Trained and tested on a large 12,570 scans from 1,845 patients dataset, CorneaNet attained area under the curve (AUC) of 0.997 for the detection of clinical KC and 0.983 for detecting subclinical KC on a held-out test set. The Grad-CAM outputs corroborated that the attention of the model is consistent with established clinical biomarkers.
