AB  - Keratoconus is a progressive eye disease recognised by thinning of the cornea with irregular curvature, and requires early identification for preventing serious visual impairment. In the paper, we have presented a system that assists with the automated detection and diagnosis of keratoconus using corneal topography maps through machine learning. The proposed approach employs three distinct Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) architectures, each tailored to analyse specific corneal planes: sagittal, corneal, and elevated. The models achieved average validation accuracies of 96%, 98%, and 91%, respectively, demonstrating the effectiveness of plane-specific feature extraction for this task. To enhance clinical interpretability, Gradient-weighted Class Activation Mapping (Grad-CAM) was integrated to visually highlight regions of the cornea most influential in the model's predictions. To validate the design decisions, alternative architectures, including MobileResNetV2, were evaluated in comparative experiments. While some alternative architectures, such as MobileNetV2, demonstrated marginally higher single-metric accuracy, the proposed system achieved a superior balance of high diagnostic performance, effective extraction of corneal morphology, and greater computational efficiency compared to larger, pre-trained models. The system is engineered to detect curvature anomalies, asymmetries, and elevation discrepancies through the corneal maps. The present model complements the classical clinical analysis by facilitating systematic scrutiny of the corneal abnormalities. The results of the study demonstrate that characterisation of multiple corneal planes adds rigour to the diagnosis of keratoconus by leveraging biomarkers observed at different levels of the keratoconus disease continuum. The study shows the potential utility of objective and scalable measures in ophthalmology for purposes of earlier screening and monitoring response to treatment.
